---
intent: >
  Widen parseBrainAide from extracting one body section (`## Prose`) to
  extracting three named body sections (`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`,
  `## Research hub`) with a strict closed vocabulary. Add `playbookHub`
  and `researchHub` fields to `ParseBrainAideResult`'s `ok` variant
  alongside `prose`. Update `BrainAideConfig` JSDoc to point at the
  three-section format. Reject unknown headings and missing required
  sections as `malformed-body`. Apply a strict-failure migration: a
  pre-widening prose-only `brain.aide` returns `malformed-body` naming
  the two missing sections rather than silently defaulting them.
---

## Project Structure

This is an additive/edit plan against an existing module. The module's
external surface stays the same in name and arity — `parseBrainAide`
(default), `parseBrainAideFromString`, and `interpolateArgs` keep their
signatures. What changes is the shape of `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` (gains
`playbookHub` and `researchHub` fields next to `prose`) and the body
grammar the parser enforces (closed vocabulary, exactly three required
sections).

```
src/service/parseBrainAide/
├── .aide                         ← [existing, aligned] intent spec — already
│                                    rewritten for the three-section body
├── plan.aide                     ← [this file, REPLACES the prior 6/6-done plan]
├── todo.aide                     ← [existing] alignment record (untouched)
├── index.ts                      ← [edit] orchestrator + helpers
│                                    exports unchanged in name and signature:
│                                      default async function parseBrainAide(root: string): Promise<ParseBrainAideResult>
│                                      export function parseBrainAideFromString(content: string): ParseBrainAideResult
│                                      export function interpolateArgs(config: BrainAideConfig): string[]
│                                    behavior changes:
│                                      - body extraction grows from one section
│                                        (`## Prose`) to three (`## Prose`,
│                                        `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`)
│                                      - body grammar is closed: unknown
│                                        top-level headings → malformed-body;
│                                        missing required sections →
│                                        malformed-body naming every absent
│                                        section
│                                      - the `ok` result gains `playbookHub`
│                                        and `researchHub` fields next to
│                                        `prose`; all three return verbatim
│                                        (byte-identical between heading and
│                                        next-heading-or-EOF, no trimming)
└── index.test.ts                 ← [edit] new tests for the three-section
                                     body, closed-vocabulary rejection,
                                     migration path; existing prose tests
                                     extend to cover the new fields
```

External edits in another file (called out for the implementor; the
`src/types/` change must land in the same step that widens the parser
because the parser's `ok` result references the central type):

```
src/types/index.ts                ← [edit] widen ParseBrainAideResult.ok
                                     to carry `playbookHub: string` and
                                     `researchHub: string` next to
                                     `prose: string`. Update the union's
                                     JSDoc to describe the closed body
                                     vocabulary and the three-section
                                     extraction. BrainAideConfig and
                                     BrainAideMcpServerConfig are
                                     unchanged.
```

Single-helper layout from the prior plan still applies:
`interpolateArgs` and the `parseBrainAideFromString` / `parseBrainAide`
pair fit comfortably in one `index.ts` and stay there.
`interpolateArgs` remains defined ABOVE the default export per the
function-ordering rule. The body-section extraction logic lives inside
`parseBrainAideFromString` (or a private helper at the top of the file
above `interpolateArgs`); no new sub-helper folder.

## Prerequisites

This plan has no external module prerequisites. Every type and constant
it touches lives in files this plan edits directly
(`src/service/parseBrainAide/` and `src/types/index.ts`). The intent
spec at `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` is already aligned to the
three-section schema (`status: aligned`).

**Downstream-breakage notice (not a prerequisite — informational):**
Widening the `ok` variant to carry `playbookHub` and `researchHub` does
NOT break existing consumers — they read `prose` only and TypeScript
allows reading a subset of fields from an object type. Tightening the
body grammar to require three sections WILL break any existing
on-disk `brain.aide` that has only `## Prose` (the pre-widening shape):
those files now return `malformed-body`. That behavior is intentional
(strict-failure migration) and is the spec's load-bearing decision.
The downstream consumer that actually reads the new fields
(`provisionBrain`) is governed by its own `plan.aide` and depends on
this plan landing first.

## Plan

### 1. Widen `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` in shared types

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Edit `src/types/index.ts`. Update the `ParseBrainAideResult` union
      so the `ok` variant carries three body fields instead of one. The
      shape becomes:

      ```
      | { kind: "ok"; config: BrainAideConfig; prose: string;
          playbookHub: string; researchHub: string }
      | { kind: "missing" }
      | { kind: "malformed-frontmatter"; reason: string }
      | { kind: "malformed-body"; reason: string }
      ```

      Keep `BrainAideConfig` and `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` exactly as
      they are — frontmatter shape does not change in this refactor.

      Update the union's JSDoc to describe the new body grammar:

      - `"ok"` — the file was found, frontmatter parsed cleanly, all
        required fields validated, and the body contained exactly the
        three required sections (`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`,
        `## Research hub`) and no unknown headings. Each body field
        carries the verbatim bytes between its heading and the next
        heading boundary (or end-of-file for the last section). No
        substitution runs on any body field.
      - `"malformed-body"` — frontmatter is valid but the body fails
        the closed-vocabulary grammar: any required section
        (`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`) is absent,
        OR any unknown top-level heading is present. `reason` names
        every missing section in deprecated-style listing OR names the
        unknown heading.

      Do not introduce per-section result subtypes. The three fields
      are siblings on the same `ok` variant; consumers read only the
      ones they own (per-consumer section ownership is enforced by the
      consumer's `.aide` spec, not by the parser's type shape).

      Confirm by grep that no consumer in `src/` currently destructures
      `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` in a way that breaks under field
      additions (e.g. `const { kind, config, prose } = result;` is
      additively safe; `const { ...rest } = result;` would over-collect
      but is not used anywhere — verify).

### 2. Add a body-grammar helper inside `parseBrainAideFromString`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [x] Edit `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`. Replace Step 5 of
      `parseBrainAideFromString` (the current single-heading
      extraction) with a closed-vocabulary three-section walker. The
      walker is a private helper named `extractBodySections` declared
      ABOVE `interpolateArgs` and the default export, per the
      function-ordering rule. Signature:

      ```
      function extractBodySections(body: string):
        | { kind: "ok"; prose: string; playbookHub: string; researchHub: string }
        | { kind: "malformed-body"; reason: string }
      ```

      Behavior:

      - **Required headings (closed set, exactly three):**
        `## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`. The
        canonical heading strings are kept as a module-level constant
        (`REQUIRED_BODY_HEADINGS = ["## Prose", "## Playbook hub",
        "## Research hub"] as const`) declared above the walker.

      - **Walk strategy.** Scan the body for every line that matches
        `^## .+$` (any top-level heading). Each match is a candidate
        section boundary. Build a list of `{ heading, startIndex }`
        entries in document order, where `startIndex` is the byte
        offset immediately after the heading line's terminating
        newline.

      - **Closed-vocabulary rejection.** If any candidate heading is
        not in the required set, return `{ kind: "malformed-body",
        reason: "unknown heading: <heading-as-written>" }`. The reason
        names the FIRST unknown heading found (left-to-right). Do not
        list multiple unknown headings in the same response — the
        parser surfaces one error per parse, and the user fixes it
        and re-runs.

      - **Required-section presence check.** After walking, if any
        required heading is missing, return
        `{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "missing required
        sections: <comma-separated list, in REQUIRED_BODY_HEADINGS
        order>" }`. List every missing section in one response so the
        user fixes them all in one edit. Format examples:
        - one missing: `"missing required sections: ## Research hub"`
        - two missing: `"missing required sections: ## Playbook hub,
          ## Research hub"`
        - all three: `"missing required sections: ## Prose,
          ## Playbook hub, ## Research hub"`

      - **Section slicing.** For each required heading found, slice
        from `startIndex` (the byte after the heading's newline) to
        the start of the next heading line — or to end-of-file if the
        section is last in document order. The slice is byte-identical
        to what the user wrote between those bounds; no trimming, no
        normalization. The single-newline strip immediately after the
        heading line that the legacy extractor applied should be
        preserved for backward-compatible test assertions on `prose`
        (the prior tests assert `expect(result.prose).toBe("\n" +
        body)` — review and decide whether to keep the strip or
        change it; if changed, update those tests in step 3).

      - **Order independence.** The three required sections may
        appear in any order in the file; the walker assigns each
        slice to its named field regardless of document position.
        (The default scaffold ships them in declaration order
        `Prose → Playbook hub → Research hub` for readability, but
        the parser does not enforce ordering.)

      Then update `parseBrainAideFromString`'s Step 5 (the part after
      successful frontmatter validation) to call `extractBodySections`
      and merge its result into the final return:

      - On `extractBodySections` returning `malformed-body`, return
        that result directly.
      - On `extractBodySections` returning `ok`, return
        `{ kind: "ok", config, prose, playbookHub, researchHub }`
        — the three section fields carry through unchanged from the
        helper's return.

      Update the function's JSDoc to describe the new body grammar and
      mention the strict closed vocabulary.

### 3. Rewrite the test suite for the three-section body

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] Edit `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts`. The structure
      stays the same (per-branch `describe` blocks, real-tempdir
      fixtures, `it.each` for parameterized cases), but every fixture
      that wrote a single-section body needs to grow to three sections,
      and new test groups land for the closed-vocabulary rejection and
      the strict-failure migration path.

      - 3a. **Update `CANONICAL_BRAIN_AIDE`** to carry the three
            required sections. Use the spec's good example as the
            byte-level reference:

            ```
            ---
            name: obsidian
            mcpServerConfig:
              command: npx
              args:
                - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
                - "D:/notes/my-vault"
            ---

            ## Prose

            <existing prose body — keep unchanged>

            ## Playbook hub

            <a non-trivial multi-line body that exercises the slicer>

            ## Research hub

            <a non-trivial multi-line body that exercises the slicer>
            ```

            The non-trivial body content for `## Playbook hub` and
            `## Research hub` should each include at least: a `#` H1
            heading inside the section (which the closed-vocabulary
            check must NOT mistake for a top-level `## ` heading),
            multiple paragraphs, and at least one nested `### `
            sub-heading. This proves the walker only treats `^## .+$`
            (exactly two leading hashes) as a section boundary, not
            `^# .+$` (one) or `^### .+$` (three).

      - 3b. **Update `CANONICAL_CONFIG`** — unchanged from the prior
            plan; frontmatter shape did not move.

      - 3c. **Extend the happy-path test (3a)** to assert on all three
            body fields:
            - `result.prose` matches `/Your brain is an Obsidian
              vault\./`
            - `result.playbookHub` equals the verbatim bytes between
              the `## Playbook hub` heading line and the
              `## Research hub` heading line (the slicer's natural
              boundary)
            - `result.researchHub` equals the verbatim bytes between
              the `## Research hub` heading line and end-of-file

            Compute these expected slices from the `CANONICAL_BRAIN_AIDE`
            constant rather than hardcoding them — a `extractCanonicalSection(name)`
            test-helper makes the assertions resilient to small body
            edits in 3a's fixture.

      - 3d. **Keep the missing-file test (3b) and malformed-YAML test
            (3c) unchanged.**

      - 3e. **Keep the missing-required-frontmatter-field test (3d)
            and the deprecated-field rejection test (3d') unchanged.**
            Their fixtures already include the canonical body; update
            those bodies to carry all three sections in step 3a's
            same fashion.

      - 3f. **Rename test 3e from "missing prose body" to "missing
            required sections (parameterized)"** and replace it with a
            parameterized `it.each` over the three required headings
            and combinations:

            - missing `## Prose` only → reason matches
              `"missing required sections: ## Prose"`
            - missing `## Playbook hub` only → reason matches
              `"missing required sections: ## Playbook hub"`
            - missing `## Research hub` only → reason matches
              `"missing required sections: ## Research hub"`
            - missing two (e.g. Prose + Research hub) → reason lists
              both in `REQUIRED_BODY_HEADINGS` order
            - missing all three → reason lists all three in order

            For each case, the fixture has valid frontmatter and a
            body that contains only the non-missing sections (plus an
            optional sentence of body text). All cases assert
            `result.kind === "malformed-body"` and the `reason`
            string matches the expected list literally — anchor on
            `REQUIRED_BODY_HEADINGS` order, not file order.

      - 3g. **Add a new test group: "unknown heading rejection".**
            Parameterized `it.each` covering:
            - body has all three required sections AND an unknown
              `## Notes` heading → `reason === "unknown heading:
              ## Notes"`
            - unknown heading appears BEFORE all required headings
              → still rejected, names the first unknown
            - unknown heading appears AFTER all required headings
              → still rejected, names the first unknown
            - case sensitivity: `## prose` (lowercase) is treated as
              an unknown heading (NOT a case-insensitive match for
              `## Prose`); `reason === "unknown heading: ## prose"`
              and the user's actual `## Prose` section is therefore
              also missing — assert ONE error fires (whichever the
              walker reaches first per its scan strategy; document
              the contract in the test assertion comment).

      - 3h. **Add a new test group: "strict-failure migration".** A
            single test that asserts the load-bearing migration
            policy:
            - Fixture: a pre-widening `brain.aide` with valid
              frontmatter and a body containing only `## Prose`
              (no `## Playbook hub`, no `## Research hub`).
            - Assertion: `result.kind === "malformed-body"` and
              `result.reason ===  "missing required sections:
              ## Playbook hub, ## Research hub"`. The parser does
              NOT silently default the missing sections to empty
              strings.

      - 3i. **Keep the verbatim-prose test (3f) structurally** but
            extend it to cover all three body fields:
            - prose containing literal `${name}` and `${rootPath}` →
              passes through byte-identical (existing assertion)
            - `## Playbook hub` containing literal `${name}` → passes
              through byte-identical
            - `## Research hub` containing literal `${name}` → passes
              through byte-identical

            This is the load-bearing per-section verbatim invariant.
            The interpolation surface is `mcpServerConfig.args` only;
            no body field ever runs through substitution.

      - 3j. **Keep the name-is-metadata test (3g) unchanged in
            behavior** but update fixtures to carry all three body
            sections.

      - 3k. **Keep the interpolation tests (3h, 3i, 3j) unchanged.**
            These exercise `mcpServerConfig.args` substitution; the
            body grammar widening does not affect them.

      - 3l. **Update the `parseBrainAideFromString` tests (3k group)**
            so all fixtures carry the three-section body. The
            `noProseContent` legacy fixture (which proves
            `malformed-body` reaches identically through the disk and
            string paths) becomes a `noPlaybookHubContent` fixture (or
            equivalent) — assert `result.kind === "malformed-body"`
            and the reason names the missing section.

      - 3m. **Add a per-section ownership documentation test (new):**
            assert that `result.config` does NOT contain `prose`,
            `playbookHub`, or `researchHub` (those are siblings of
            `config` on the result, not properties of `config`).
            Documents the type boundary visibly so a future consumer
            does not reach into the wrong nesting level.

### 4. Verify the parser in isolation, then surface downstream breakage

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] 4a. Run `npm test -- parseBrainAide` (or the equivalent vitest
      filter scoped to this module's test file). Every test in
      `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts` must pass. If any
      fails, fix the parser code (not the test) — the spec is the
      contract; the tests assert the contract; failing tests mean
      the implementation drifted from the spec. Do NOT relax
      assertions to make tests pass.

      Likely failure modes:
      - The slicer mishandles a body where one of the required
        headings is the LAST section (no successor heading to
        bound the slice). The slice should run to end-of-file,
        not return empty. Fix: when computing the upper bound,
        fall back to `body.length` when the candidate is the last
        in document order.
      - The walker treats `^# ` or `^### ` as a section boundary.
        Fix: tighten the regex to `^## .+$` (exactly two hashes
        followed by space and at least one character).
      - The `## Prose` body sentinel-phrase test fails because
        the body's strip-newline behavior changed. Either restore
        the prior single-newline strip, OR update the test
        expectation; document the choice in the Decisions section
        of this plan if the strip behavior changes.

- [x] 4b. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` from the project root. The parser's
      own files must compile cleanly. Downstream consumers
      (`provisionBrain` and the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` tests)
      WILL fail to compile or run because they have not yet been
      updated to (a) source body bytes from the new `playbookHub`
      and `researchHub` fields, and (b) carry the new sections in
      the bundled template. Record the list of failing files in
      the implementor's hand-off message; do NOT modify those
      files. They are governed by their own plan.aide files (the
      orchestrator runs them next in the dependency chain).

      The only file inside this plan's scope that may surface
      `tsc` errors is `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` itself
      (or its test file). Both must compile; if not, the type
      widening in step 1 or the helper in step 2 needs another
      pass.

## Decisions

- **Body fields are siblings on the `ok` variant, not nested under
  `config`.** The parsed `BrainAideConfig` is the typed frontmatter
  only; body sections are a separate concern with separate semantics
  (verbatim, never interpolated, per-consumer ownership). Putting
  them on `config` would conflate "things sync writes to .mcp.json"
  with "things the install service scaffolds to disk", which is
  exactly the conflation the named-section shape exists to prevent.
  A consumer reading `result.config.mcpServerConfig` cannot
  accidentally see playbook-hub bytes; a consumer reading
  `result.playbookHub` cannot accidentally see frontmatter. The
  field-name boundary is the per-consumer ownership contract,
  visible in every consumer's import line.

- **The body grammar is closed and the rejection lists every missing
  section in one error.** The spec's good examples explicitly show
  `"missing required sections: ## Playbook hub, ## Research hub"`
  as a single response listing both stale fields, and the parser
  surfaces them all in one parse so the user fixes them all in one
  edit. This matches the deprecated-frontmatter-field pattern from
  the prior cycle (`"deprecated fields: rootPath, tools"`) — same
  one-pass-many-failures shape, applied to the body grammar.
  Unknown headings, by contrast, surface one-at-a-time because the
  unknown-heading scan halts at the first violation (the user
  removes it and re-runs); listing multiple unknowns would invite
  the parser to keep walking past a failed grammar check, which
  the closed-vocabulary rule rejects.

- **Required headings live in a module-level constant, not a magic
  string.** `REQUIRED_BODY_HEADINGS = ["## Prose", "## Playbook
  hub", "## Research hub"] as const` is the single source of truth.
  The walker iterates it; the missing-section reason builder
  iterates it; tests assert against it. Any future change (a new
  required section, a heading-text rename) is a one-line edit.
  Hardcoding the strings inline at every call site would
  decentralize exactly the grammar this module exists to centralize.

- **Section ordering is not enforced.** The walker matches by
  heading text, not document position. A user who reorders their
  sections (e.g. puts `## Research hub` first because they edited
  that section last) gets a clean parse. The default scaffold
  ships them in declaration order for readability, but ordering
  is a content-author convention, not a parser constraint. This
  matches the spec's "the parser walks the body once after
  frontmatter validation, locates each heading at the start of a
  line, and slices each section from its heading boundary..."
  language — extraction is by heading lookup, not by index.

- **No content-validation on body sections.** The parser checks
  that each required section is present and that no unknown
  headings appear; it does NOT check that `## Playbook hub`
  contains a table or that `## Research hub` contains specific
  text. Body content is the user's domain; the parser's domain is
  body-section-presence and closed-vocabulary enforcement. This
  is the section-level analogue of the schema-minimum-equals-
  maximum frontmatter rule.

- **No new sub-helper folder for the body walker.** The
  `extractBodySections` private helper plus the existing
  `parseBrainAideFromString` / `parseBrainAide` / `interpolateArgs`
  trio still fit comfortably under ~250 lines. The progressive-
  disclosure single-helper exception still applies. If a future
  refactor adds environment-variable expansion to the
  interpolation engine, factor each responsibility into its own
  subfolder at that point — not preemptively.

- **The strict-failure migration is intentional and tested
  explicitly.** A pre-widening `brain.aide` (one section, prose
  only) returns `malformed-body` naming the two missing sections.
  No silent default fills them in; no `aide_upgrade` carve-out
  auto-migrates. The user hand-edits their file to add the
  sections, copying the new defaults from the bundled scaffold.
  This matches the spec's load-bearing decision and prevents the
  package from authoring content the user is supposed to own. A
  dedicated test in step 3h asserts this behavior so a future
  refactor that introduces silent defaults will fail visibly.

- **Type widening lands in the same plan as the parser change.**
  The `ParseBrainAideResult` shape lives in `src/types/index.ts`,
  one layer above the parser, but the parser is the only writer
  of the new fields. Splitting the type widening into a separate
  plan would force the parser to either (a) reference the legacy
  type and break, or (b) reference the new type before it lands
  and break. Co-locating the type widening with the parser
  rewrite means both land atomically and the parser's tests
  exercise the new shape immediately. The widening is additive
  (new optional-by-construction fields on a discriminated union
  variant), so consumers that read only `prose` continue to
  compile.

- **The disk read path is unchanged.** `parseBrainAide(root)`
  still reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` — the path move from the
  prior cycle is preserved. This plan does not touch the file
  location, only the body grammar inside it.

- **Step 4b records but does not fix downstream breakage.** The
  two downstream modules (`provisionBrain`, `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`)
  each have their own `plan.aide` for the body-widening cascade.
  Fixing them inside this plan would expand its scope past the
  single-module rule. The verify step's job is to make the
  breakage visible — the orchestrator picks up those plans next.
